WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:11.290
Genes, genomes and DNA: these words have slipped
into our daily news cycles and our awareness,

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:15.219
but what they actually mean often remains
unclear.

00:00:15.219 --> 00:00:19.660
In these lessons, we aim to give you, not
just the explanation of what a genome is,

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.320
but a real-life perspective on why you already
care about genomics – perhaps in ways you

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.540
didn’t even realize.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.200
What is a genome?

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:33.239
A genome contains all of the information that
a cell needs to develop, function, and reproduce

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:37.949
itself, and all the information needed for
those cells to come together to form a person,

00:00:37.949 --> 00:00:39.989
plant, or animal.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:44.339
Genomes contain an organism’s complete set
of genes and even tinier genetic structures

00:00:44.339 --> 00:00:47.959
that help regulate when and how those genes
are used.

00:00:47.959 --> 00:00:52.499
The ability to regrow a torn ligament, the
clues that might predict the onset of mental

00:00:52.499 --> 00:00:57.249
illness, the nutritional potential of crops,
and even the history of life itself are all

00:00:57.249 --> 00:00:59.299
encoded in genomes.

00:00:59.299 --> 00:01:04.850
Genomics is a new science and it tries to
understand all the genetic material that we

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:10.770
have, the genes, as well as tiny structures
that regulate where a gene is turned on, how

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:15.920
active the gene is, and how it contributes
to overall function.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.430
To begin in genomics, everything starts with
DNA.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:23.549
DNA is the fundamental building block of all
of life.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.859
DNA is a complex molecule.

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:31.729
Think of DNA as a set of letters that form
sentences and eventually these sentences form

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:34.990
paragraphs and these paragraphs form whole
books.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:40.460
So, we have DNA, and then we have genes that
are made up of pieces of DNA, and then as

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:44.880
we assemble all the genes together, we have
our book of life, the genome.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.619
Genomics is very much technology driven.

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:53.189
We have benefited from an incredibly fast
development of the technology.

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:56.450
The core of genomics lies in sequencing.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:02.439
It has been amazing to watch the technology
develop very rapidly over the last 30 years.

00:02:02.439 --> 00:02:06.719
It's not that long ago that we started with
a very, very tedious process that was done

00:02:06.719 --> 00:02:12.330
in individual labs that took hours and hours
to obtain a sequence of maybe 20 or 30 nucleotides

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.769
or pieces of the DNA.

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:19.750
Now it's possible to sequence an entire human
genome, which is 3 billion such pieces, in

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.610
a few hours.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:25.990
We've gone through several forms of technology,
and we've also gone through changes in the

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:29.150
organization of the science as a result.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:34.090
In the early days, everything was done in
individual labs, contributing in small amounts

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.860
to the sequencing that we were able to analyze
collectively.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:42.739
Now we are moving more and more as the technology
advances to centralized facilities with very

00:02:42.739 --> 00:02:48.099
expensive equipment that able to handle large
amounts of DNA and perform amazing feats of

00:02:48.099 --> 00:02:49.099
sequencing.

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:53.510
Genomics is not just based on sequencing technology.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:58.330
It is actually spawned a whole other area,
which is called bioinformatics.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:03.310
Since genomics gathered such huge amounts
of data, it's impossible to use conventional,

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:08.799
statistical approaches, and general data organizing
approaches to handle it all.

00:03:08.799 --> 00:03:15.060
As a result, we have a new breed of scientists,
who is expert in biology, expert in computation,

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.630
and to be able to bridge the gap and give
us the tools we need to make sense of the

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.019
data.

00:03:20.019 --> 00:03:25.349
These scientists, bioinformaticians, are incredibly
essential to the whole genomics enterprise.

